---
// How nnxyz drew: what the recorded stroke order of 548 drawings shows, and
// twelve works drawn at five points in their build. Numbers come from
// curation/analysis/strokes/ (method strokes-v1); nothing is a duration,
// because the archive records order and not time.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import BuildSequence from '../components/BuildSequence.astro';
import { byId, artworkPath, displayTitle } from '../lib/catalog';
import { placement, exhibitionPath } from '../lib/exhibitions';
import { formatUtcDate } from '../lib/dates';
import {
  measures,
  strokeSummary as s,
  techniqueWorks,
  techniqueMethod,
  percent,
} from '../lib/technique';
const all = s.all;
const by = s.byCollection as Record<string, typeof s.all>;
const collections = [
  ['nifty-faces', 'Faces'],
  ['nnotb', 'New Nifty on the Block'],
  ['rawblocks', 'Rawblocks'],
  ['nnxyz-other', 'Other nnxyz'],
] as const;
const tenths = all.decileMedians;
const width = (x: number) => (x * 1000).toFixed(0);
const sessions = s.sessions.all;
const busiest = sessions.busiestDays.slice(0, 5);
const works = techniqueWorks.map((entry) => {
  const work = byId.get(entry.artworkId)!;
  const m = measures.get(entry.artworkId)!;
  const pair = entry.pairWith ? byId.get(entry.pairWith)! : null;
  const pairMeasures = entry.pairWith ? measures.get(entry.pairWith)! : null;
  const place = placement(entry.artworkId);
  return { entry, work, m, pair, pairMeasures, place };
});
---

<Layout
  title="Technique — The nnxyz Rooms"
  description="How nnxyz drew: what the recorded order of every stroke in 548 drawings shows, and twelve works shown at five points in their build."
>
  <article class="text-page technique-page">
    <h1>Technique</h1>
    <p class="story-summary">
      How the drawings were built, from the one record this collection has that
      no gallery of paintings does: the order, width, color and transparency of
      every stroke.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nifty Ink kept every stroke of every drawing in the order it was made. The
      replay on each artwork page follows that order, and so can a measurement.
      This page reads the stroke record of all {all.works} Nifty Ink drawings by
      nnxyz in the collection, then shows twelve of them at five points in their
      build. The archive stores no timing, so nothing here is a duration or a
      speed; "first" and "last" mean order. The board's own background pass,
      which {all.worksWithHelperPass} of the drawings carry, is counted apart
      from the hand's strokes.
    </p>

    <h2 id="findings">What the record shows</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>The hand starts with line.</strong> The board lays the ground, and
      the first drawn stroke after it covers almost nothing: the median first
      stroke covers {percent(all.firstStrokeCoverageMedian)} of the canvas, and
      only {percent(all.groundFirstShare)} of the drawings open with a stroke
      that covers more than a quarter of it. nnxyz does not block in a big shape
      and refine it. The drawing begins as line, and the mass accumulates.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Broad to fine, every time.</strong> Across the sequence the median
      stroke narrows from {width(tenths.widthMedian[0])} thousandths of the
      canvas side in the first tenth to {width(tenths.widthMedian[9])} in the
      last, and the median stroke shortens from{' '}
      {tenths.lengthMedian[0].toFixed(1)} canvas sides to{' '}
      {tenths.lengthMedian[9].toFixed(2)}. The last tenth is dots and short
      marks: the highlights, the teeth, the signature. Every series follows the
      same curve.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Burying is the method.</strong> The share of each new stroke that
      lands on paint already laid rises from {percent(tenths.overpaintMean[0])}{' '}
      in the first tenth to {percent(tenths.overpaintMean[9])} in the last. By
      the midpoint nearly every mark goes over an earlier one. The Faces are the
      most buried and fill the square to{' '}
      {percent(by['nifty-faces'].finalCoverageMedian)}; the later series leave
      more of the ground showing.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Paint arrives with the portraits.</strong> Translucent strokes are{' '}
      {percent(by['nifty-faces'].translucentShareMedian)} of a typical Face and{' '}
      {percent(by.rawblocks.translucentShareMedian)} of a typical Rawblocks. The
      two peaks by month are January 2022, the pale winter portraits, and
      November 2022, the Rawblocks heads. The summer masks of 2022 are almost
      entirely opaque.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Dark grounds have seasons.</strong> {percent(all.darkGroundShare)}{' '}
      of all grounds are dark. December 2021 is the darkest month, then May
      2022; from July 2023 on the ground is almost never dark.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Sessions.</strong> {sessions.activeDays} drawing days over three
      years, a median of {sessions.worksPerActiveDayMedian} works on a working
      day, and a longest run of {sessions.longestStreakDays} consecutive days.
      The busiest days were{' '}
      {busiest.map(([day, n], i) => (
        <>
          {formatUtcDate(String(day))} ({n})
          {i < busiest.length - 2
            ? ', '
            : i === busiest.length - 2
              ? ' and '
              : ''}
        </>
      ))}
      . By hour the record peaks in the late evening and is quietest in the
      small hours of UTC, which is an evening practice in Central European time.
    </p>

    <h2 id="build-order">The build, in tenths</h2>
    <p>
      Medians across all {all.works} drawings for each tenth of the stroke
      order: how much of the canvas has paint, how wide the typical stroke is,
      how many colors have appeared, and how much of each stroke lands on
      earlier paint.
    </p>
    <div class="technique-table-wrap">
      <table class="technique-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Tenth</th>
            <th scope="col">Canvas covered</th>
            <th scope="col">Stroke width ‰</th>
            <th scope="col">Colors so far</th>
            <th scope="col">On earlier paint</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {tenths.coverage.map((c, i) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{i + 1}</th>
              <td>{percent(c)}</td>
              <td>{width(tenths.widthMedian[i])}</td>
              <td>{tenths.paletteSize[i]}</td>
              <td>{percent(tenths.overpaintMean[i])}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="technique-table-wrap">
      <table class="technique-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Series</th>
            <th scope="col">Works</th>
            <th scope="col">Strokes (median)</th>
            <th scope="col">Dark grounds</th>
            <th scope="col">Canvas covered</th>
            <th scope="col">On earlier paint</th>
            <th scope="col">Translucent</th>
            <th scope="col">Colors</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {collections.map(([id, title]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{title}</th>
              <td>{by[id].works}</td>
              <td>{Math.round(by[id].strokesMedian)}</td>
              <td>{percent(by[id].darkGroundShare)}</td>
              <td>{percent(by[id].finalCoverageMedian)}</td>
              <td>{percent(by[id].overpaintMeanMedian)}</td>
              <td>{percent(by[id].translucentShareMedian)}</td>
              <td>{Math.round(by[id].distinctColorsMedian)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="twelve">Twelve drawings, five frames each</h2>
    <p>
      Each work below is drawn from its native SVG at five points in its stroke
      order: after a tenth, a quarter, a half and three quarters of the hand's
      strokes, and complete. The background pass is in every frame. The reading
      under each says what the hand did between the frames; the numbers are the
      record's.
    </p>
    {works.map(({ entry, work, m, pair, pairMeasures, place }) => (
      <section class="technique-work" id={`work-${work.inkId}`}>
        <h3>
          <a href={artworkPath(work.inkId)}>{displayTitle(work)}</a>
          {pair && (
            <>
              {' '}
              and <a href={artworkPath(pair.inkId)}>{displayTitle(pair)}</a>
            </>
          )}
        </h3>
        <p class="technique-why">
          {entry.why} Hangs in{' '}
          <a href={exhibitionPath(place.room.id)}>{place.room.title}</a>.
        </p>
        <BuildSequence work={work} strokes={m.strokes} />
        {pair && pairMeasures && (
          <BuildSequence work={pair} strokes={pairMeasures.strokes} />
        )}
        {entry.reading && <p class="technique-reading">{entry.reading}</p>}
        <p class="technique-facts">
          {m.strokes} drawn strokes
          {m.helperStrokes > 0 && (
            <>
              {' '}
              after {m.helperStrokes} background pass
              {m.helperStrokes > 1 ? 'es' : ''}
            </>
          )}
          , {m.distinctColors} colors, {m.translucentStrokes} translucent
          strokes; {percent(m.finalCoverage)} of the canvas covered when
          complete, {percent(m.overpaintMean)} of each stroke on earlier paint
          {pairMeasures && (
            <>
              ; the second drawing {pairMeasures.strokes} strokes,{' '}
              {pairMeasures.distinctColors} colors,{' '}
              {pairMeasures.translucentStrokes} translucent
            </>
          )}
          . Recorded {formatUtcDate(m.recordedAt)}.
        </p>
      </section>
    ))}

    <h2 id="method">Method</h2>
    <p class="story-method">{techniqueMethod}</p>
    <p class="story-method">
      Coverage and overpainting are measured on a 160 by 160 raster of the
      stroke paths with round caps, with transparency ignored for coverage.
      Widths and lengths are divided by the shorter canvas side so works of
      different sizes compare. A stroke counts as buried when more than 90% of
      its pixels were already painted. The measures and the summaries are kept
      with the curation record, with the hash of every file read.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/story/">The story →</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/">Return to the collection →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
  <script>
    import { initSequences } from '../scripts/sequence';
    initSequences();
  </script>
</Layout>
